title: Reverting onto the current state
tags: [[The operation log]]
kind: reference
audience: dev
status: proposed
summary: A revert applies each operation's inverse to the state as it is now — whatever was written since — so it needs no dependency check and no complete log, and its own record composes exactly.

A revert of a set of operations applies, newest first, each one's ''inverse to the current
state''. What the operation wrote is not checked against what is there now: for a field set
A → B and now holding C, the revert writes A. A revert means "go back to what this
changed", not "undo this if nothing touched it since".

!! The inverse of each operation

|!Operation |!Its inverse, applied now |
|`set_field` (and the watcher's `file_*`, set-field-shaped) |set the field back to the rows it replaced |
|`append_field` |remove one row of that name holding that value — not the whole field, which would erase what was appended since |
|`delete_field` |put the removed rows back |
|`create_metarecord` |delete the metarecord, whatever it holds now |
|`delete_metarecord` |recreate it, under its own uuid, with the fields it had |
|`set_metarecord` |set the whole field set back |

So "back to A" holds for a set; for a multi-map it is the delta that is undone, not the
cell that is restored.

!! What it removes

No dependency analysis (doc "Operation dependencies"): nothing is "blocked by" a later
operation, so nothing needs the operations between a target and now to be in the log. That
is what lets retention trim anything ([[The operation log]]).

!! What can still fail

Ordinary write validation, not a structural rule: recreating a metarecord whose uuid
exists again, a `TreeRef` that would make a cycle, a schema violation. The revert fails as
the equivalent write would, and says why.

!! The one real risk, and its warning

Reverting an old operation silently overwrites a newer one on the same field: rating 3 →
5, then 5 → 4, then reverting the first gives 3 and the 4 is gone. For an explicit revert
this is what was asked. What the operation wrote is kept to ''warn'': the plan says "this
field holds C, not B, since operation N". Undo, which takes the newest change, almost never
meets the case.

!! Composition

A revert records what it really found — C, not B — as its own "before". Reverting the
revert puts C back: undo then redo returns exactly to the state between them, whatever
was written in between.

!! Files

Reverting a move A → B whose file is now at C moves it from C to A; the file side of a
revert (doc "Filesystem coordination") already does that, and the plan shows it. A content
the trash does not hold cannot come back: the metadata is reverted, the truth written
again by the watcher.

!! Identities

* ''A metarecord keeps its uuid'': recreating a deleted one restores it. A uuid is unique by construction, so it cannot collide.
* ''A field row does not keep its id'': a row put back gets a new one. Restoring the old id (as a navigation did) could collide with rows created since — ids are allocated by the store, not unique by construction. Clients holding a row id re-read on the change anyway ([[Daemon data in panels]]).
* Hence an `append_field` is undone ''by value'', not by row id: after undo, redo, undo, the value it appended sits in a row the redo created, under another id. Two rows of the same name and value are interchangeable — the metarecord's content, and its version, are the same whichever goes (doc "Metarecord version").
